[{:seon.task/name "a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a step that throws still answers what it took. Root's reader of Flow's error channel forwards the message the step took (`::flow/msg`) as :msg; `seon.root/back-op`'s :error branch answers it through `seon.root/answer-taken` (its :dest through back-op itself, its :p or :route, its :rid; an answer a proc took is answered again on [pid :answers], never one already an error); the api keeps what each step took in `::taken` (a volatile Flow's revert keeps), moves it to `::lost` when the step threw and answers all of it at its defect; the render proc takes an error answer to its view request without rendering it. `seon.root/state`, `seon.root/errors` and root's agent-entry deref plainly, and `state` answers a defect undecorated; `seon.web`'s ask! parks on its promise. Replaced: four (deref ... 5000 {}) fallbacks (so `seon.server/agents-answer` reported no agents while root was merely busy), fs-call!'s 30 s :timeout, web's 10 s nil, and `seon.render.proc/ASK-BOUND-MS` with its re-ask. Left: `my.root/problems` does not branch on `seon.root/errors` answering a defect; the writer's and the agent procs' own batches (a writer step that throws past its own catch names only its first request; an agent proc's frames name their rid as :id or :k, which `answer-taken` does not read); Flow's error channel is a sliding(100) buffer, so a defect dropped there is never answered. A timed deref left behind is `bin/laws clocks`' finding."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-throwing-step-answers-every-pending-request
                      seon.procs-test/root-state-never-answers-an-empty-system}}
 {:seon.task/name "no-head-of-line-blocking"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the api writes an agent's [agent :in] with Flow's blocking send (64 slots plus a 1 MB socket, `seon.agent-proc`). Reproduced: one busy agent plus one chatty agent stalled root's whole api about 25 s while health said :running. Correct: the api never blocks on one agent (the agent proc holds overflow in its state and admits what fits); health reports progress (the oldest unanswered request), not liveness."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-busy-agent-never-stalls-another}}
 {:seon.task/name "shutdown-and-death-are-dataflow"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: shutdown is nested time bounds and SIGKILL ladders with nothing recorded (`seon.root/stop!`: `(deref p (+ ap/stop-bound-ms 1000) ::late)`, `(deref p 15000 :timeout)`; `seon.agent-proc/stop-bound-ms`); child death is seen only by the fork server's 100 ms idle poll (`sock/recv-within! conn 100` in `seon.fork-server`), so deaths during a commit stream and after a failover are lost and a promoted server leaves a zombie. Correct: child death is an event (kqueue EVFILT_PROC, or a SIGCHLD self-pipe in the fork server's poll); a death is recorded at socket EOF, its exit status added when known; spawn and clone are refused while closing; root parks on the drain; an agent that does not drain is a recorded problem; the conn and LMDB env belong to the writer. Deletes the time bounds, the kill ladders and `seon.agent-proc/stop-bound-ms`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-death-is-recorded-at-once-and-its-status-added
                      seon.procs-test/shutdown-drains-without-a-kill}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-clock"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: time decides in two places outside a declared clock: the stall check is a go block started inside the api's step (`(a/timeout (quot (:stall-ms st) 2))` in `seon.root/api-proc`), and the rate bucket is a callback take! on `a/timeout` (`seon.fx`). The dashboard has no beat (`seon.render.proc` renders on an event, an answer or a tab's request). Correct: one declared :clock proc emits ticks on bounded channels; the stall and rate bounds are config keys, each named in docs/clocks.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-clock-is-the-only-time-source}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-threads-are-a-known-number"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.check/declared-threads` names every thread root starts and the number each owner owns; bin/check lifecycle, scale and threads-declared assert the exact roster after each cycle (`seon.check/thread-diff`). The drift once seen (root's OS threads 25 -> 29 over 10 start!/stop! cycles, while Jolt's threads stayed flat) was Chez's parallel-GC sweepers: setup_sweepers (chez c/gc.c:2988) starts one per thread a collection finds waiting and keeps it, so the count followed load. Our Chez (jolt/gc-sweepers) adds (collect_sweepers n), and root fixes the pool right after forking the fork server (`seon.lib/gc-sweepers!` with `:seon.config.root/gc-sweepers`). Jolt's runtime threads are named (jolt-carrier-N, jolt-timer, jolt-shutdown); put!/take! that cannot complete are channel waiters on fibers, not a thread each (Jolt c508734d); render is :mixed, not on the :compute pool."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-main-resumed"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every boot of `seon.root/start!` resumes the main `(cfg/value :seon.config.root/main)` names (default.edn `#or [#env SEON_MAIN \"main\"]`); a new store creates it from the seed. A run's record is main's commit history. bin/check's steps set SEON_MAIN=check-<step>. A resumed main's reconciler reaps the earlier boot's run and starts the next one, so the proof asserts the first boot's run is still there, not the run count. Replaced: a main per boot named `run-<time>-<id>` (`:seon.config.root/run-name` and its minting, deleted), which left the standing store with 68 mains and every restart starting over. Design: the archived paper PLAN-branches.md. (git history)"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/a-second-boot-resumes-the-same-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-end-of-a-root-is-a-fact"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.root/stop!` first writes `:seon.root/stopped {:boot :cause :at-ms}` on main, durable (`:flush? true`; cause `:stop!` unless the caller names it), and a refused stop fact still stops root (its answer carries it); `seon.check/root-serve` names its cause: `:stdin-eof` (bin/root stop closes the holder's pipe) or `:signal` (SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP through Jolt's shutdown hooks, which wait for the stop); `seon.root/start!` writes `:seon.root/previous-end` with its boot fact (`seon.root/previous-end`): the previous boot's stop fact, or `:unclean` when it left none. bin/check durable-read asserts a SIGKILLed root ended `:unclean`. Wiki: docs/parity.md#operating-the-system and docs/failure-points.md. Replaced: a root's end leaving nothing on main, so a kept run could not tell a clean stop from a SIGKILL or a crash."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/every-end-of-a-root-is-a-fact}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-error-is-the-one-error-map"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an error is `seon.contract/error` alone, carrying only members that describe it (`:agent`, `:behind`, `:uncontracted`, a red run's `:tests`/`:reports` under a typed `:seon.merge/red`), and every asker branches on `contract/error?`. A request no live agent can take answers `seon.root/no-agent`'s error map; the agent view's `:error` and the `{:op :failed :error e}` sent to an agent hold the error map; the run stores the error as it is (`:seon.run/error` is a `:map`). What v1 did: one error map (`seon.error`). Replaced (about 25 lines): fabricated protocol members on errors (an eval answered `[{:id -1 :tag :err ...}]`, tests `:pass 0 :fail 0 :error 1`, `:merged? false`, `{:ok false}` merged into every writer error), a second `{:error \"string\"}` shape, and the readers' patches (`(= -1 (:id (first r)))`, `seon.reconcile/error-text` stripping members back out, `(false? (:merged? r))`). Not this task: `seon.reconcile`'s `(:error end)` is the end's `:seon.end/error` field; the `:error` of `:seon.root/source` and `:seon.root/generation` are stored fact fields."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-refused-answer-is-the-one-error-map seon.reconcile-test/an-agent-dead-in-its-turn-loop-records-its-cause seon.problems-test/an-agent-death-is-recorded-with-its-cause seon.problems-test/root-records-what-went-wrong-in-an-agent seon.agent-test/an-agent-that-throws-ends-with-its-cause}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-keeps-what-is-used"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (each item a delete or move; existing tests and bin/check stay green): the agent ping route is gone (step/run-loop's control ping stays); the check-only wrappers (`pause-agent!`, `resume-agent!`, `agent-health`, `health`) are gone; the agent-status sets are read from the schema (`seon.root/gone-statuses` by `contract/properties`, `seon.render.ui` by `contract/enum-values`); `seon.root/previous-end` pulls root's entity by its lookup ref; the fork server's stop is one private release!; `seon.reconcile/pull-end` answers nil for no end; a clone refused for too many agents says so. Left: method dispatch keys decoded with `clojure.core/read-string` (`seon.program`, `seon.stage/stage!`) beside `seon.edn/decode` decode through seon.edn; `fs-call!` and `agent-stats!` went with `seon.main/drivers-leave-src`; lib's `summ`, `gc-stats`, `sysmem` are only check's or cost's and stay; an ext request of a kind root does not know is answered refused."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-refused-answer-is-the-one-error-map seon.reconcile-test/a-run-without-an-end-reads-nil}}
 {:seon.task/name "start-takes-a-config-layer"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.root/start!` takes `:max-running`, `:provider` and `:stall-ms` as options beside the config, and `seon.config/with-provider` folds a provider into the config written on main's root entity; bin/check (`:max-running 2 :stall-ms 1000`) and seon.e2e pass them. The written config is what the reconciler reads (main's root fact), so nothing bypasses main; outside the one resolver are the process's own reads: `seon.config/value` reads the process-wide `seon.config/config` (loaded once with its --set layers), so two roots in one process (the checks') cannot differ through it. Correct: a root's config in force is a layer the resolver reads (start! takes `{:set {k v}}`, resolved like a --set but per root; every reader in root's procs resolves through the root's config, not the process's); the options and with-provider go. A design change: before building, root's gate (AGENTS §3) asks which existing mechanism carries a per-root layer and what v1 did (a config per system)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-keeps-what-it-shows-by-config"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root's view keeps an agent's newest 20 evaluations and the newest 20 root errors by literal `take-last 20` (`seon.root/set-agent`'s :evals-add, the :root-errors fold in `seon.root/back-op`), and `seon.render.ai` says \"holds the newest 20\": a bound that is not config (AGENTS §5). Correct: each bound a `:seon.config.root` key, read where it is used, and the render's text reads the same key."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-keeps-evaluations-by-config}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-spawn-answers-at-its-agents-ready"
  :seon.task/plan "Root, 2026-10-23, from rename's bin/test under load (4 shards): seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them's first eval answered \"no live agent :a1\" right after the spawn, and seon.agent-test/in-agent asks {:op :agents} up to 300 times 0.2 s apart until its agent is idle, a poll (AGENTS section 9; task seon.check/attach-answers-when-its-agent-is-ready, which this takes). Cause: the api's :spawn (seon.root/api-proc) answers {:id} at once, while the agent is :spawning; nothing answers when it is ready, so a caller polls, and one that sends at once can find the agent already gone. Correct, at the api, the one place every spawn goes through (seon.root/spawn!, seon.server's attach, the dashboard, the reconciler): the :spawn's caller is answered {:id} at its agent's hello (set-agent's {:ready true}, the event root's :ready is), never at once; an agent that ends before it is ready answers that end instead: stopped or killed, the stop; died, seon.root's died error with the exit status the fork server reaped (record-death: the process's exit, not a timeout). So an eval to an agent sent after its spawn's answer goes to a live, ready agent, and attach answers once its agent is ready. The reconciler takes a death at spawn as the run's death (its row started, naming the agent, then its dead path), not a refused spawn. in-agent loses its loop. Proofs: seon.procs-test/a-spawn-is-answered-at-its-agents-ready (withheld while :spawning, {:id} at :ready), seon.procs-test/a-spawn-whose-agent-dies-first-answers-the-death (the died error with the reaped exit status); seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them beside a full bin/test with in-agent's loop gone, red before, green after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-spawn-is-answered-at-its-agents-ready seon.procs-test/a-spawn-whose-agent-dies-first-answers-the-death seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-cron-proc-puts-scheduled-work-on-a-queue"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: AGENTS §4 names the cron proc as one of the three places time may decide (it only puts a message on a queue at a scheduled time); `resources/seon/system.edn` has no such proc, and scheduled work (v1's `seon.schedule`) is missing (docs/parity.md#tasks-merges-tests). A working prototype exists only outside this repository: `flowlab/r7_cron_door_merge.clj` in the tag `history/prototype` and the `cron-step` in `flowlab/kit.clj` in the same tag, recorded in the outer repository's the archived paper option3-FLOWLAB-2.md (git history). What correct looks like: one step-fn proc declared in system.edn whose only effect is to put a task's release (or a message) on its queue at the time a schedule entity on main names; its next time is a recorded fact; nothing else waits on a clock. Named in docs/clocks.md under the owner's exceptions when built."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-scheduled-release-is-queued-at-its-time}}
 {:seon.task/name "roots-log-is-bounded"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `bin/root start` sends a detached root's output to runs/root.log (bin/root), appended across every start for the checkout's life; nothing bounds or rotates it. Correct: when bin/root starts a root and the log is past a config key's bound (bytes, its unit and provenance in resources/seon/config/default.edn), it moves the log to root.log.1, replacing the one before, so a checkout keeps the current log and one previous. Rotation happens at start, an event, never on a timer. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/a-start-past-the-bound-keeps-one-previous-log}}
 {:seon.task/name "operator-commands"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `bin/root` has status, start, stop and next; one root per checkout on its own ports (`bin/ports`); its output goes to runs/root.log. v1's bin/seon also had open (the dashboard in the browser), logs (the root's log) and reset --force (the running root unlinks its main and forks a fresh one, keeping every cache; ~/src/seon/script/seon/operator.clj). Correct: `bin/root open` opens the checkout's dashboard URL; `bin/root logs` follows runs/root.log; `bin/root reset` restarts the root on a new main of the same store (`:seon.config.root/main`), the old main kept as history and nothing deleted (deleting a record is the owner's act). Each a line in bin/root's usage and docs/machine.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/reset-starts-a-new-main-and-keeps-the-old}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-prunes-old-generations-and-check-logs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: nothing prunes `target/check/` (each check run's logs) or a root's old generations (under `:seon.config.source/generations-dir`, each a worktree and a binary of about 200 MB, made by `seon.fx/build` with bin/worktree --at); deleting them is manual, keeping the running generation and the one before (docs/machine.md#disk-stays-bounded). Correct: root's merge, after the :build effect answers, removes every generation but the running one and the one before (bin/worktree --remove-at), and removes check logs past a config key's count of runs; a store holding a kept run is never removed (runs are evidence; reclaiming them is the owner's act). Deletes the manual step from docs/machine.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-build-leaves-the-running-generation-and-the-one-before}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-boots-without-a-synthetic-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.root/start!` seeds no program by default (:datoms 0), and boot no longer depends on a seed (the warm-up that queried app.m0/f1 is gone). `seon.db/seed-tx` (18 namespaces app.m0..app.m17 of 20 uncontracted defns each, with held app.mN/tidy tasks) is written only where a check asks for it (`seon.check`'s smoke, robust and scale pass :datoms 2000). Before, start! defaulted to :datoms 2000, so the standing root and every check forked agents holding a program nobody wrote."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.main-test/a-fresh-root-holds-no-program-it-was-not-given
                      seon.main-test/warm-needs-no-seed}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-compute-executor-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "Jolt's Flow runs :compute steps on `clojure.core.async.impl.dispatch`'s compute-executor (reference-code/jolt/stdlib/clojure/core/async/impl/dispatch.clj:60), an unbounded newCachedThreadPool of unnamed daemon threads with a 60 s keep-alive, and bounds each step with .get compute-timeout-ms 5000 (flow/impl.clj:419): a step past 5 s throws into root's errors while its worker keeps running, and the next step takes another worker. No proc in src declares :workload :compute today (`seon.render.proc/render-proc` is :mixed), so the hypothesis that it explains root's unattributed threads (lifecycle 25 -> 29 over cycles, scale 100 root threads 38 -> 55) is unproven. Correct: first prove or refute it (name the threads, or count compute workers before and after each cycle). If it holds, root passes create-flow a declared :compute-exec (a fixed pool of named threads, its size a seon.config key) and a step's bound becomes an observed overrun (its duration recorded), not an abandoned future. Estimate: root's thread count after a cycle equals the declared sum, exactly."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares
                      seon.render-test/a-slow-render-step-is-recorded-not-abandoned}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-is-answered-and-failover-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "(1) Done: `seon.root/agent-health` and `seon.root/health`, which answered :unknown past 1000 ms, are deleted (task seon.check/every-wait-is-the-event-it-means); `seon.check`'s one ping records a proc that does not answer as :blocked. (2) Open: the fork server's :standby-failed event (`seon.fork-server`, forwarded to the api) reaches `seon.root/back-op`, whose :ev case has no branch for it and falls through to the unchanged state: dropped. The server then has no standby, and its next death is :fork-server-lost with no recorded cause. Correct: a ping's answer passes through as itself (an error map stays an error map); :unknown only for a proc observed dead; a failed standby is a recorded platform problem (:seon.fork-server/standby-failed with :active-threads), and the view shows the server has no standby."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-ping-of-a-gone-agent-answers-its-error
                      seon.procs-test/a-failed-standby-is-a-recorded-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:seon.config.agent/stall-ms` (30000, a guessed tolerance, now a config key; it was a var) is the silence after which a busy agent is observed stalled once; `seon.root/start!` takes :stall-ms, and the api's step schedules its stall check with a go block on `(a/timeout (quot stall-ms 2))` in seon.root. A 25 s compute shows nothing while a legitimate long proof run reads as stalled. Correct: a request carries its declared bound (the form's, task seon.turn/a-form-has-a-time-bound; a test run's is its tick budget, `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks`) and the agent proc observes a stall when that bound passes with no frame: one recorded :seon.agent/stalled naming the bound, no second number; the ticks come from the one :clock proc (task seon.root/one-clock). Deletes stall-ms, the :stall-ms option and the go-block timer. The flusher's sleep this task also named is gone (durability is continuous group commit, task seon.writer/durability-is-continuous-group-commit)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/one-clock" "seon.turn/a-form-has-a-time-bound" "seon.agent/one-test-bound-derived-and-named"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-stall-is-observed-past-the-requests-own-bound
                      seon.procs-test/the-flusher-waits-on-the-clock}}
 {:seon.task/name "event-loss-is-counted"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's buffers are literals: `seon.root/start!`'s events-in is `(a/sliding-buffer 8192)`, `seon.root/events` taps a sliding 1024 that is never untapped, and the render proc's tap is a sliding 4096 (seon.render.proc). A sliding buffer drops the oldest with no count, so the dashboard can miss events and nothing says so. Correct (the step-fn law: buffers bounded by their loss semantics): every buffer is declared in resources/seon/system.edn :chan-opts with its loss; a sliding buffer counts its drops into the api's view (:events-dropped by tap), shown on the dashboard; c/events taps are released with their reader. Estimate: one counter per drop."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-dropped-event-is-counted
                      seon.procs-test/root-buffers-come-from-the-declaration}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-sweeper-pool-is-fixed-before-anything-collects"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: `seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares` read {:gc-sweepers [0 1]} (agent-loop saw [0 2]) on main in some checkouts and not others, and green an hour earlier. Cause: Chez (our fork's gc.c, `S_collect_sweepers`, jolt/gc-sweepers) starts one sweeper thread per thread waiting in a collection and never stops one; `seon.root/start!` fixed the pool at `:seon.config.root/gc-sweepers` (0) only after the store was opened, the program loaded and the fork server forked, and any collection before that, beside a waiting thread (Datahike's, the carriers'), started sweepers the fix cannot stop. Measured: `./seon sweepers-held` (six allocating threads, 30 collections, a fresh binary) ends at 6 sweepers when nothing fixed the pool first; a boot's own count was 0 to 4 across identical runs. Correct: `seon.main/-main` fixes the pool at 0 as its first act, before anything allocates beside another thread; root then fixes it at the configured number after the fork server forked (n > 0 must start after the fork: AGENTS section 4, only a process that never started sweepers may fork). So the roster declares exactly the configured number, and the fork server forks with none started at boot. Left open: with `:seon.config.root/gc-sweepers` above 0, a fork server restart (seon.root :fork-server-down) forks while root's n sweepers run; the default 0 does not. Cost: nothing measured (30 collections beside six threads: 6-16 ms real with six sweepers, 14-16 ms with none; docs/failure-points.md: sweepers did not shorten root's small collections). Replaces nothing; the Chez gap (a running sweeper is never stopped) stays documented in `seon.lib/gc-sweepers!`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/the-sweeper-pool-is-fixed-before-anything-collects seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares}}
 {:seon.task/name "warm-exercises-the-render-and-request-path"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.root/warm!` does not exercise d/with-reads, the render proc's comp-entry or the conversation's request path, so a forked process's first render and first turn are cold. Correct: warm! runs each once on a seed value before the fork server forks. Proof: a forked agent's first turn costs what its second does (ticks), measured."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "one-merge-path"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-25 (docs/decisions.md, root's special cases go): a branch reaches main only through a merge task, whose run the reconciler judges and merges with the writer's octopus merge (`seon.reconcile`'s :octopus-merge, `seon.writer/octopus-merge`). What existed beside it: `seon.root/merge!` (its request-table case, the api's :merge-tests op, `seon.agent-proc`'s gated :merge-tests branch that forked tests then asked the writer's :merge, the writer's :merge op and `merge-branch`, the api's `{:merged ...}` answer and `:merged` event with its render count and stream row (`seon.render.ui/merge-words`), the `:seon.merge/red` error): a second way to change main that skips the done rule, `:behind` handling and the octopus's disjointness, with no production caller. Its users were the smoke, scale and durable check modes and web_test. Deleted all of it (about 70 lines of src). The checks put a branch into main with the one writer op the reconciler sends (`seon.check`'s merge-in!: the agent commits its branch, `seon.agent/commit!`, as a run's end is committed before its merge, then an octopus merge of one member; without the commit durable-write merged nothing, the agent's eval still uncommitted); smoke's `gated by its tests` became `a1-merged` (its tests are already its own check). Correctness: one merge mechanism, the one the reconciler judges. Perf: none on a running root (the path had no caller); the checks' merges no longer fork a child to run tests first (one test fork per merge replaced by one commit eval, in smoke, scale and durable). Proved: bin/prove, bin/check smoke, durable, scale 12 (ten merges), bin/test seon.agent-test seon.root-test green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/calls-become-requests seon.web-test/a-refused-ui-call-reaches-the-stream-and-main
                      seon.procs-test/a-writers-merge-names-its-runtime seon.reconcile-test/the-judge-and-the-merge-gate-agree-on-contract-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). seon.root/await-db parks on a promise that only a later commit satisfying pred delivers (src/seon/root.clj:1013); its callers add a defect after t as a second end (seon_done, src/seon/server.clj:151-170), and test/seon/await.clj's await-quiet rides on it. When root is quiet (no message in flight, every proc parked: the reconciler's :seon.root/quiescent report) and pred does not hold, nothing inside root will ever make it hold, and the wait hangs its caller, the caller's socket and, in a test, the shard (instability 2: 1h45m at 0% CPU). Root stopping does not end it either. Correct, at the producer: root keeps its open waits (await-db's watch keys) and answers each, at the quiescence report that finds pred false, with a typed error {:seon.error/type :seon.root/never-true :t t :quiet-t q} (a fact, never a clock); and at stop! with :seon.root/stopped. A caller that waits on the outside world (a file change, a peer's commit) says so and is not answered at quiet (an explicit :outside? flag, the one exception). seon_done then answers a judge that never finished as that error. judge! (root.clj:1011) puts with >!! on a 64-slot channel and parks forever on a dead, full consumer: the same rule, a put to a proc that died answers its error (see seon.root/a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error). Proofs: an await-db whose pred cannot hold on a quiet root answers :seon.root/never-true; one waiting at stop! answers :seon.root/stopped; seon_done on a run whose judge's agent died answers the error, never parks."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). >!! onto a bounded channel whose consumer died parks the putter forever once the buffer is full: seon.root/judge! (src/seon/root.clj:1011, 64 slots), src/seon/web.clj:146,148,152,171 toward the web proc, src/seon/fork_server.clj:175-183's event reader into ev-ch. A Flow proc that dies leaves its in-channels open. Correct: a proc's death closes its in-channels (Flow's own stop closes them; a step that threw is the defect path), so a put returns false and the putter records the dead proc as its answer (AGENTS section 4: a dependency is a channel a proc parks on; its death is a fact). Proof: a proc stopped with a full in-channel; a >!! to it returns false and its caller answers an error naming the proc."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-named-wait-ends-when-a-runs-work-throws"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the agent loop's worktree session: with seon.turn/begin! writing a session's run on its branch without its task (fixed in jolt/agent-loop 89dca5cf3), a session's second run threw in seon.turn/work! (seon.context/task-data's contract, its task nil), and both stage tests' named waits (seon.root/await-db sys \"the implement parked\" ...; agent_test a-requests-implement-continues-its-designs-request and a-waiting-run-parks-and-a-message-wakes-the-same-process) waited six hours: root's problems held the eval error, yet the named wait, which must end at the reconciler's quiet with :seon.root/quiet-before-true, never ended. Reproduction: revert 89dca5cf3's change to begin! and run bin/test seon.agent-test. Cause not yet found: a work! answered with :ex goes on to :judge and :branch-diff (seon.reconcile :worked), so either one of those requests is never answered on a run whose work! threw, or the run's end never reaches quiet. Correct: the run ends (its :worked-error as its reason) and the wait answers quiet-before-true; a test that waits on a fact the run cannot make goes red, never hangs."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-run-whose-work-throws-ends-and-the-wait-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-running-roots-os-thread-count-is-what-it-declares"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by jolt/fork-guard, 2026-10-25: seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares is red while running, on main's binary as on the branch: the OS counts more threads than the roster plus the sweepers, {:os [43 79]} {:os [43 79]} {:os [43 47]} (main, fbbc502e2) and {:os [43 84]} {:os [43 50]} {:os [43 48]} (branch, load 12-18). Every program thread is in the roster by name (jolt.host/user-thread-names lists only async-mixed-N and seon-fs-events, as declared), and after each stop! the roster is exact. The excess shrinks across cycles, so the suspect is threads that have ended but the kernel still counts (the lag seon.check/cycles records rather than compares after a stop: 164 of 200 trials), here those boot and the agents' first evaluations end. Find what they are first (their count against the threads joined since boot), then either compare only what the program can answer for while running, as after a stop, or end what lingers at its owner. Proof: the test green 3 of 3 under load. Green under the fork server's boot order (jolt/zygote-one-mode's named run: root's threads start only after the fork server is forked): check it on main once that branch merges, before working this."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares}
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-gone-agent-stays-gone-and-every-request-is-answered"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen live: root's view held an agent :busy with no process (main too), and an eval sent as it ended hung. Causes, each fixed at its producer: (A) seon.agent-proc/agent-in took a :req after the process ended and reported :status :busy, which seon.root/set-agent let overwrite :dead (it kept only a stopped status), re-arming the stall check; and seon.root/end-agent answered only the proc's in-flight list, never a request the api sent in the step the agent ended. Now a gone status stays gone (a late :busy is not news), and end-agent answers every request to the agent the api holds (a :prove, which the test service answers, aside), whether it reached the proc before its end, after it, or never. (B) the fork server's :exit was matched by pid over every agent ever seen, so a reused pid sent it to an ended agent's entry and the death waiting on it never got its exit: now the death waiting on that pid first, then a live agent, then the last one to end with it and no exit yet (a stopped agent's exit is still recorded). (C) a root that crashed or was stopped wrote no end for its agents, so main kept them live: seon.task/earlier-agents-tx writes :dead for an earlier boot's agent still shown live. (D) an eval before the hello made the agent :busy and its spawn was never answered: the hello answers the spawn whatever its status."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-late-live-status-leaves-a-gone-agent-gone seon.problems-test/an-ending-agents-every-request-is-answered
                      seon.problems-test/a-stopped-agents-exit-is-recorded-on-it seon.problems-test/an-exit-goes-to-the-death-waiting-on-its-pid
                      seon.problems-test/a-new-boot-ends-an-earlier-boots-live-agents seon.problems-test/the-hello-answers-the-spawn-whatever-its-status}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner: we may have merge conflicts or agents failing to merge, so make sure the tools to fix them by hand on root's side work as expected. On a worktree root of one's own (never runs/store), stage each failure and repair it from root's REPL (seon_eval root, or the server's :eval and :attach ops) with only the tools that exist, as root would at 3 a.m.: (1) two branches change one var differently and the merge refuses (:seon.merge/unmerged) or its merge task cannot resolve it: list the conflicts (:seon.run/conflicts, my.root/problems, root's page), resolve (attach to the held merge task, then my.branch/unmerged and my.branch/resolve! :theirs or a hand redefinition), get it merged; (2) a run green on its branch whose merged program is red: find it from root, read why, send it back or fix it; (3) a run stuck :behind main, or a held task: see it on root's page, release or rerun it (seon.root/release!, my.root/run!, my.root/cancel!); (4) a write-back to git that fails (:seon.run/commit-error): see it, fix the cause, retry. Staging: the scripted scenarios in seon.fixtures/scenarios (:hairy and :conflict for 1, :semantic for 2, :hairy's held merges for 3), booted as test/seon/e2e.clj's scenario mode boots a scripted root; 4 by moving or locking the merges branch. Known before staging: root has no conflict-resolving function of its own; my.branch/unmerged, resolve! and merge-main! run in an agent's process, so root's path is to attach to the held merge task (whether that works end to end is finding 1's question). State: staged and repaired once on a worktree root (2026-10-27), every form and reply in docs/merging.md: conflicts resolve by attaching to the held merge task and resolve! :theirs; a held task releases with my.root/run!; red-after-merge is caught (the writer refuses a branch behind main). Gaps found, most severe first: (1) every worktree root writes back to the shared jolt/seon-merges ref (seon.fx/git-commit; one branch name per checkout, or refuse a ref another worktree has checked out); (2) a failed write-back is lost and never retried, the run reads :merged and no root cause names it (a my.root/write-back!, and a merged run with a :seon.run/commit-error as a root cause); (3) a merge stays :behind forever when main gained a namespace (the branch carries the form with :ns nil; seon.merge/merge-tx, program-of), and (4) nothing bounds :behind reruns (they count as green, so max-merge-turns never sees them); (5) attach leaves the task's model \"outside\" for good, so its next run idles; (6) no tool takes a running run back (run! refuses, release! does not check, cancel! is final; a my.root/hold!); (7) a behind merge branch is judged before merging main (\"proof-unknown\" instead of \"merge main first\"); (8) cancel leaves the task's problem rows open, and the page counts closed rows; (9) the page shows root causes only when quiet, my.root/problems allocates 0.1-1.7 GB a call on ~25 tasks, a request with raw newlines answers one contract error per line, a retried write-back keeps its old error. Next step: report these to root (done), then fix each at its cause on small branches (1 and 2 first), file the big ones, and write the working forms into docs/merging.md as root's playbook, one section per failure, each form runnable so the docs test can check it. Also staged: record per failure the forms root typed, what came back and whether root's page showed it; report the findings before fixing; fix each wrong or missing tool at its cause on a small branch (or file it if big); write the working forms into docs/merging.md as root's playbook, one section per failure, each form runnable so the docs test can check it."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "await-db-has-one-arity"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md, S8; still true: seon.root/await-db has an unnamed arity ended only by a commit and a named one that also ends on the reconciler's quiet, src/seon/root.clj:1134-1175). A caller chooses whether the fact it waits on is the reconciler's work, and a named wait can answer :seon.root/quiet-before-true while another proc (the vector proc embedding) can still commit. Correct: one wait that ends when nothing in the system can still commit, from the quiet facts every proc reports (the reconciler's, the vector proc's, the problems report's :quiescent), no flag and no second arity. Replaces the two arities and the reconciler's held-waits bookkeeping beside root's quiescence report (seon.reconcile/quiet). Do with seon.root/a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true. Proof to write: a named wait during an embedding does not answer quiet-before-true."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-attached-run-is-taken-back-by-root"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 5 and 6 of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl (staged 2026-10-27). (5) `seon.server/attach!` writes the task's own model as outside (task/env-tx id {:model outside}, server.clj:88) and nothing restores it: after seon_done the task still reads :seon.task/model outside, so a later run of it (a :behind respawn) idles with nothing driving it and quiet never comes. Correct: outside is the run's env only, or :done restores the task's model. (6) Root has no function that stops a run and keeps its task: `my.root/run!` refuses a running task, `seon.root/release!` writes :running without a check (a no-op there) and `my.root/cancel!` (root.clj:62) is final. Correct: `my.root/hold!`, execution :held by compare-and-swap from :running, killing the live run as hold-tx does, so root takes a looping task back without losing it. Proof to write: after done on an attached run the task reads its own model; hold! on a running task leaves it :held with its run ended."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-behind-merge-ends-in-a-fact"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 3, 4 and 7 of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl (staged 2026-10-27, to reproduce first: stage fixtures :conflict, attach the merge task, merge main and resolve, then give main a new namespace with seon.source/text-tx). (a) A merge stays :behind forever after main gains a namespace: the form of a namespace new on main reaches the branch with :ns nil, so main differs at that key on every merge (`seon.merge/program-of` :ns read, merge.clj:197, or the carry in `seon.merge/merge-tx`, merge.clj:275); control: a change to an existing namespace merges on the second run. Fix at the carry; check a namespace made from an agent's REPL (the :newns scenario) the same way. (b) Nothing bounds :behind reruns: a :behind run ends green so max-merge-turns never counts it (reconcile.clj:993 ends the run :behind and the task stays ready); under a model that loops forever and root causes (quiescence only) never names it. A :behind of a branch that already merged main at the head it was refused at is a defect fact, and the task is held with that evidence. (c) A behind merge branch is judged before main is merged: an attached agent's REPL runs main's program while its branch db holds the old base, so the proofs pass on the branch and the held reason reads proof-unknown. seon_done (or the judge) refuses a branch whose base is behind with the writer's :seon.merge/behind message and its fix before judging proofs, or attach to a merge task runs my.branch/merge-main! as the turn loop does. Proof to write: merge-main! followed by the octopus merge is not :behind when main gained a namespace; a :behind rerun at an unchanged head holds the task."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "root-repair-reads-are-cheap"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 9 and the smaller ones of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl: root's repair reads cost and say too little. (1) `my.root/problems` took 66 ms / 100 MB to 1,063 ms / 1.7 GB allocated on a store of about 25 tasks and `seon.server/task-summaries` about 560 ms / 850-915 MB a call. (2) The page names root causes only at quiescence, so a run that never ends shows 'a31 idle' and nothing links a cause to its run or task; the conflicts' content is not on the page. (3) A held merge appears twice in `my.root/problems` (:merge and :failed), and the footer counts closed rows. (4) After a successful write-back retry the run keeps its stale :seon.run/commit-error (retract it); a namespace with no file records a commit-error naming each key twice (reconcile.clj:526-531, unfiled-error). (5) A request with raw newlines answers one :seon.server/defect per line (server.clj:238-260): a line that does not read as a map answers once, naming the rule one request per line, newlines escaped. Proof to write: contracted calls and bytes of one my.root/problems read on 25 tasks, flat in the conversation (a cost proof); one answer for a multi-line request."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "root-holds-no-test-code"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-29 (heap census of an idle scratch root, Chez compute-size by exclusion): the 81 *-test namespaces, seon.check, seon.e2e, test-runner, seon.test-store and clojure.test(.check) are loaded into root's dynamic heap, 114 MB exclusive (147 MB with the drivers): 1,660 test functions in generation 4 beside the 1,636 src functions in the static image. Root never runs a deftest (tests run in agents' test children and bin/test's shards), so they should cost root 0. Learn first: what loads test/ in root (main's program holds test/ forms because :seon.config.source/dirs imports them for reach digests, seon.source; a form main holds is loaded at boot) and whether agents and test children rely on inheriting them. Correct: root imports test/ as facts (reach, digests) but does not evaluate test namespaces; the fork server loads them after it is forked, so every agent still inherits them as shared, read-only pages (a read costs no RAM: docs/cost.md#how-we-measure). Measure root's live heap and footprint before and after on e2e calc and the standing store; a forked agent's test run must not compile test/ again."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-view-keeps-handles-not-values"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-29 (heap census): root's view of an agent keeps its last :seon.config.root/view-window evals (seon.root/set-agent, :evals-add) with their result values: 20 MB for one agent's probe evals; and root's *e reached 78 MB. AGENTS section 4: a kept value is a reference. Correct: the view keeps each eval's handle and its printed head (what a render shows), never its value; *e in root keeps the error's data, never a Throwable. Proof: root's view after evals returning a 10 MB value holds bytes independent of the value's size (a cost proof over value size)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
